---
title: "Self-Employed? How Lenders Really Look at Your Income"
description: "Self-employed borrowers often earn well but show low taxable income. Here's how lenders assess your income and how to set yourself up to qualify."
publishDate: 2026-11-18
category: refinancing
readTime: 5
---

If you run your own business, you already know the frustration: you earn good money, you've been in business for years, and then a lender looks at your tax return and says you don't qualify. The reason is usually simple. Good accounting minimizes taxable income, and taxable income is what most lenders look at first.

## What Most Lenders Want to See

* **Two years of personal tax returns and Notices of Assessment.** Many lenders average your last two years of income.
* **Proof your taxes are paid,** with no balance owing to the CRA.
* **Business documents,** such as financial statements, business registration or articles of incorporation.
* **A track record.** Two years in business is a common benchmark, though some lenders will work with less if you're in the same line of work.

## The Write-Off Trade-Off

Every legitimate expense you deduct lowers your tax bill. It also lowers the income a lender can use. If you're planning to buy or refinance in the next year or two, it's worth talking with your accountant and your mortgage broker *together* about how your next returns will look. Sometimes reporting a bit more income for a year or two costs less than the higher rate you'd pay for a harder-to-qualify mortgage.

**Incorporated?** Income you leave in your corporation may not show up on your personal return. Some lenders can consider retained earnings or business financials, but policies vary widely. This is a big reason to work with a broker who knows which lenders do this.

## Your Options If the Bank Says No

* **Lenders with self-employed programs:** some A lenders have specific programs for business owners.
* **Alternative lenders:** may use bank statements or a "reasonable" stated income, typically with a larger down payment and a higher rate.
* **Private lenders:** focus on equity, useful as a short-term bridge while you build up the paperwork for a better lender.

## How to Prepare

1. File your taxes on time and keep your CRA account paid up.
2. Keep business and personal banking separate.
3. Keep your credit clean, with low card balances and no missed payments.
4. Talk to a broker six to twelve months before you plan to buy or refinance.

